k 1 he  says that a good will is a will by which  we seek to live rightly and virtuously  in Latin recta monastic way  Viva reign and some people might quibble  and say oh it doesn't actually say  virtuously  but when we look at what it means to be  ho Ness day you know that's an adverb  how its living its living in accordance  with the four cardinal virtues so it's  pretty clear that that is an integral  part of it and that's not all that the  the goodwill does the goodwill also  wants to reach the heights of wisdom  that is in Latin odd Summum sap ENT  impaired van narrator to penetrate up to  the very heights or the very highest  points of wisdom so there's you could  say a kind of cognitive and conative  thing you know action-oriented and you  know there's also an affective side to  this as well  so there's there's a lot going on here a  good will is you could say kind of a  nexus within the human person of not  just doing the right thing choosing the  right thing but having the right motives  and valuing things properly so it's it's  quite complex what is this going to look  like in you know practical life well you  know we can get a bit of understanding  by looking at what he contrast this  against a will that is fundamentally  damaged screwed up getting things wrong  a little bit earlier in in the the work  he talks about how it's up to us it's up  to our will and our free choice of the  will whether or not we give into desire  or whether we you know choose something  else than than desiring temporal Goods  so he says there's a punishment if we  screw up what is the punishment it's not  going down to hell and getting poked  with you know pitchforks by demons and  flames or anything like that it's being  the screwed up thing that we are it's  having a damaged will that is unable to  really we'll what it ought to will so he  says the punishment is the mind being  ruled by passion being robbed of its  store of virtue being dragged to their  and their poor and needy now judging  false for true now defending now  attacking what before it approved and  then running off into false fresh  falsehood with holding its assent and  often frightened of clear reasoning  despairing of finding any truth at all  and clinging closely to the darkness of  its folly and then falling back to  exhaustion this is all sort of cognitive  stuff and then he says the passions rage  like tyrants throw into confusion the  whole soul in life of men who with  storms from every quarter fear on one  side desire on another on another  anxiety or false empty joy here pain for  the thing which was loved and lost  they are eagerness to win what is not  possessed their grief for an injury  received here burning desire to avenge  it and he goes on and on and on right  and he says that this is this is what  happens to a will that is not a good  will so what makes a will a good will if  we want to avoid that sort of internal  turmoil part of it is actually valuing  the goodwill  highly that is to the extent that it  should be valued and taking a great joy  in possessing it when we actually do  possess the virtues and a right way of  motivating ourselves when even when  we're just on the way moving away from  all of these vices and passions and  cognitive problems for going the right  way we can we might feel sad about you  know how screwed up we are but we can  feel good about the the what you know  measure of goodwill we do possess the  direction that we're going in we value  that more highly than anything else any  temporal thing and we  we take joy in it he says that is really  central to the the conception of the  goodwill this is what what he means by a  will by which we live to seek  righteously and virtuously and to reach  the height of wisdom he says I want you  to tell me how much you think is will is  worth do you think that riches or honors  or bodily pleasures or all of these  together bear any comparison with it and  the vodeo says no not at all very easy  for us though to think that well if I do  the wrong thing right now in order to  make a buck or in order to get some  pleasure I'll make up for it later you  know but we've already abandoned the  goodwill in doing that so he says do you  think that those who lack this joy  suffer a small loss and being deprived  of such a good and the vodiane says no  they suffer a great loss what's really  key to Augustine's way of looking at  things is that it is up to us whether or  not we have or keep the goodwill it's  it's our choice now you know very rarely  do we do we sort of lay it out for  ourselves and mental images and say aha  the goodwill or this pleasure right we  could do that if we were you know  thinking things through well but more  often we're like doing the right thing  exercising this virtue of prudence or  temperance or or justice and making a  couple extra bucks or getting an honor  or something like that you know which  usually involves doing something that we  know is too wrong like you know telling  a lie or maybe not even a complete lie  just puffing up our accomplishments or  letting a person believe something that  isn't strictly true we could go on and  on and on with examples right so it is  up to us he says he says it lies in the  power of our will whether we enjoy or  lack this great and true good why  what is so fully in the power of the  will as the we  itself it's up to the will whether or  not it's going to be a good will or a  bad will if it has a status of being a  goodwill it's up to us whether it keeps  being a goodwill and this does in fact  require the virtues and exercising those  virtues so it does require prudence  knowing what's you know good for us or  bad for us and putting that into  practice justice getting to everybody  what what they properly are owed you  know right order is part of that  temperance you know not giving in to to  all the desires and pleasures that we  might and then courage standing up  against fear and despising the some of  the things that we otherwise would be  very into so you know to go on we do in  fact as he says choose and keep the  goodwill but he goes a bit further to  have the goodwill strictly speaking  means to love the goodwill to feel a  kind of love towards oneself or that  part of oneself that not only does  choosing not only has this capacity to  like you know decide things and order  them and prioritize them but is properly  ordered so when we have this within  ourselves it's not like we should feel  you know proud in the bad sense but we  can look at and say wow that's really  something good it may not be perfect but  it is indeed something good that we  could take some some joy in so he says  this is what it means to have goodwill  there's a kind of reflexivity to it too  he says whether to love one's own  goodwill and value it as highly as we've  said is it self goodwill that's the  question and of odious says yes that is  indeed in when you have the goodwill  part of what it means to have the  goodwill is that that goodwill values  that goodwill in the right way and that  may be a little bit complex but it's  it's you know we can see a sort of  circularity  there or reflexivity as we said it is  also going to involve loving other thing  so among them it will involve loving the  eternal law and the eternal law is this  you know thing that Augustine thinks  provides moral norms for all other laws  it's the law coming from God it's also a  law that we're able to grasp by reason  it doesn't change from time to time and  place to place situation a situation it  remains right it remains the standard of  right and so having a good will means  loving the eternal law it also means  deserving happiness in accordance with  the eternal law as well and following  the eternal law because it is the  eternal law or put putting it another  way because it's right because it's  what's good for us so the eternal law  provides a set of standards that the  goodwill can use to structure itself to  guide itself and it even sort of  ratifies the fact that part of the  eternal law is if it doesn't do that  then it's gonna fall into misery  you know the eternal law dictates that  reward and Punishment happened through  happiness and misery and that the will  gets to decide between those so what is  this ultimately going to mean it's not  just about the eternal law it's not just  about looking within it'll also have to  do with how we arrange all of the other  goods in our life the temporal goods  which are in fact genuine goods pleasure  social status our relationships with  other people  well those are those sorts of things  what do we make of them if we have a  goodwill we make sure that the goodwill  always comes first and those things if  we have to choose between them and the  goodwill it comes second  but if we can enjoy them and use them  properly then that is actually the  goodwill and they can in fact according  to Augustine be used properly that is in  accordance with the eternal law so this  is a particularly robot  and reflexive conception of what it  means to possess a genuinely good will  as a human being as somebody who is  endowed with this faculty as part of our  very human nature and one thing I'll say  by way of closing this off is that in  other places Agustin talks about the  will itself as being a very great good  that we would not be as good as we are  even though we're very often quite bad  and screwed up if we lacked a free will  so this you know the right use of the  free will the good will is something  that's going to be of absolute  importance in Augustine's moral theory  you
 

